//! Implementation of [`TaskContext`]

/// Task Context
#[derive(Copy, Clone)]
#[repr(C)]
pub struct TaskContext {
    /// return address ( e.g. __restore ) of __switch ASM function
    ra: usize,
    /// kernel stack pointer of app
    sp: usize,
    /// callee saved registers:  s 0..11
    s: [usize; 12],
}

impl TaskContext {
    /// init task context
    pub fn zero_init() -> Self {
        Self {
            ra: 0,
            sp: 0,
            s: [0; 12],
        }
    }

    /// set task context {__restore ASM funciton, kernel stack, s_0..12 }
    pub fn goto_restore(kstack_ptr: usize) -> Self {
        unsafe extern "C" {
            unsafe fn __restore();
        }
        Self {
            ra: linker_symbol_addr!(__restore),
            sp: kstack_ptr,
            s: [0; 12],
        }
    }
}

The user asks: "UPSafeCell 如何不可变借用" — how does UPSafeCell do immutable borrow (i.e., how does one take an immutable borrow from it)?

Context: rCore-Tutorial-v3. `UPSafeCell<T>` is defined in `os/src/sync/up.rs`:

```rust
use core::cell::{RefCell, RefMut};

/// Wrap a static data structure inside it so that we are
/// able to access it without any `unsafe`.
///
/// We should only use it in uniprocessor.
///
/// In order to get mutable reference of inner data, call
/// `exclusive_access`.
pub struct UPSafeCell<T> {
    /// inner data
    inner: RefCell<T>,
}

unsafe impl<T> Sync for UPSafeCell<T> {}

impl<T> UPSafeCell<T> {
    /// User is responsible to guarantee that inner struct is only used in
    /// uniprocessor.
    pub unsafe fn new(value: T) -> Self {
        Self { inner: RefCell::new(value) }
    }
    /// Panic if the data has been borrowed.
    pub fn exclusive_access(&self) -> RefMut<'_, T> {
        self.inner.borrow_mut()
    }
}
```

So in the tutorial's version, `UPSafeCell` only exposes `exclusive_access()` returning `RefMut`, which is the mutable borrow. There's no `borrow()` method. So "如何不可变借用" — the answer: in the tutorial's textbook version there is NO immutable borrow API; `UPSafeCell` deliberately only provides `exclusive_access` (a `&mut`-like `RefMut`). If you want an immutable borrow, you can:

1. Use `RefMut` — `RefMut` derefs to `&mut T`, and you can reborrow as `&T` implicitly: `let inner = cell.exclusive_access();` then use it in read-only fashion (e.g. `inner.field`), but note it's still an exclusive borrow: runtime borrow flag is set to "mutably borrowed", so any concurrent `exclusive_access()` will panic even if you intended a read. That's the key point about UPSafeCell: it's not a "reader-writer" cell, it's an exclusive-access-only cell. The rationale: on a uniprocessor with interrupts / trap-based kernel, allowing shared borrows would let you hold a `&T` across a context switch / interrupt, which is exactly the dangling-aliasing hazard they want to prevent. So they force exclusive access.

2. Add a `borrow()` method yourself:

```rust
pub fn borrow(&self) -> Ref<'_, T> {
    self.inner.borrow()
}
```

But you must be careful: `Ref` is `!Send`/`!Sync`-ish concerns — actually `Ref<'_, T>` is `Sync` if T: Sync... hmm. Actually `RefCell<T>` is not `Sync` unless manually, and `Ref`/`RefMut` are `!Send`? Let me recall: `Ref<'b, T>` has `impl<T: ?Sized> !Send for Ref<'_, T>`? Actually in std, `Ref<'b, T>` and `RefMut<'b, T>` are not `Send` (they contain `&Cell<BorrowFlag>`), yes: `Ref` contains `borrow: &'b Cell<BorrowFlag>`, and `Cell` is `!Sync`, so `&Cell` is `!Send`. So `Ref` is `!Send`. That matters if you want to hold it across... whatever.

